package JavaBasicIntro;

class Demo {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;

    void show() {
        System.out.println("a = " + a + ", b = " + b);
    }
}

class Test {
    Demo d;   // instance variable (class-level)

    // initialize object
    void init() {
        d = new Demo();
    }

    // use the same object in another method
    void use() {
        System.out.println("Using Demo object:");
        d.show();
    }
}

public class MainApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();

        t.init();   // object created here
        t.use();    // object reused here
    }
}
We store objects in instance variables so that they can be reused across multiple methods,
 since local variables are limited to method scope.

